\title{Non-Existence of Non-Constant Blend Colorings\\
on Strongly Connected Weighted Digraphs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-04}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study \emph{blend colorings} of finite edge-weighted directed graphs:
assignments of a real value to each vertex such that every vertex's value equals
the weighted convex combination of the values of the vertices it points to.
Modeling the graph by a row-stochastic weight matrix $w$ (so that the outgoing
weights at each vertex form a probability distribution), we prove that on a
finite \emph{strongly connected} digraph every blend coloring is constant;
equivalently, no blend coloring assigns two vertices different values. The proof
is a discrete maximum principle: at a vertex attaining the global maximum value,
the equality case of the convex combination forces every positive-weight
successor to also attain the maximum, and strong connectivity propagates this to
the whole graph. This is a self-contained, elementary form of the classical
statement that the only harmonic functions of a finite irreducible Markov chain
are the constants (a discrete Liouville theorem). We record three companion
results: a vector-valued generalization obtained coordinatewise; a sharpness
example showing strong connectivity is necessary; and the specialization to the
directed $n$-cycle, an explicit infinite family of strongly connected instances.
We also frame quantitative and geometric extensions.
\end{abstract}

\textbf{Keywords:} blend coloring, harmonic function, discrete maximum principle,
row-stochastic matrix, strong connectivity, irreducible Markov chain, directed
cycle, discrete Liouville theorem, convex combination.

\section{Introduction}

A recurring theme across analysis, probability, and combinatorics is that a
\emph{local averaging constraint} combined with \emph{global connectivity}
forces global uniformity. The Laplace equation forbids interior maxima of
harmonic functions; a finite irreducible Markov chain has only constant harmonic
functions; heat flows until temperature is even. This paper isolates the purely
combinatorial core of these phenomena and proves it from first principles.

We work with a finite directed graph carrying nonnegative edge weights that are
\emph{row-stochastic}: the weights leaving each vertex sum to one. A \emph{blend
coloring} assigns to each vertex a real number equal to the weighted average of
the numbers on the vertices it points to. Our central question, framed as a
non-existence problem, is: \emph{when does a finite edge-weighted digraph admit a
non-constant blend coloring?} Our main theorem answers this completely for
strongly connected graphs: it does not. The condition is sharp, and requires
neither symmetry nor reversibility of the weights.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
  \item A main collapse theorem: on a finite strongly connected row-stochastic
        digraph, every blend coloring is constant (\S\ref{sec:main}).
  \item A local maximum principle: maximality propagates along positive-weight
        arcs (\S\ref{sec:local}).
  \item A non-existence phrasing: no blend coloring takes two distinct values.
  \item Sharpness: strong connectivity is necessary (\S\ref{sec:sharp}).
  \item The directed cycle family (\S\ref{sec:cycle}).
  \item A vector-valued generalization (\S\ref{sec:vector}).
  \item Quantitative and geometric outlook (\S\ref{sec:algo}--\S\ref{sec:future}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $V$ is a finite nonempty set of \emph{vertices}.

\begin{definition}[Weighted digraph]
A \emph{weighted digraph} on $V$ is a function $w : V \times V \to \RR$ with
$w(i,j) \ge 0$ for all $i,j$. We interpret $w(i,j)$ as the weight of the arc
$i \to j$; the \emph{out-neighbors} of $i$ are the vertices $j$ with $w(i,j) > 0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Row-stochastic]
The weighting $w$ is \emph{row-stochastic} if for every vertex $i$,
$\sum_{j \in V} w(i,j) = 1$. Thus the outgoing weights at each vertex form a
probability distribution, and any weighted sum $\sum_j w(i,j)\, x_j$ is a convex
combination of the $x_j$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Arc relation]
The \emph{arc relation} $\Arc_w \subseteq V \times V$ is defined by
$i \mathbin{\Arc_w} j \iff w(i,j) > 0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Strong connectivity]
$w$ is \emph{strongly connected} if for every ordered pair $(i,j)$ there is a
directed walk of positive-weight arcs from $i$ to $j$; formally, $j$ is reachable
from $i$ under the reflexive--transitive closure of $\Arc_w$: either $i = j$, or
there is a finite chain $i = v_0 \to v_1 \to \dots \to v_k = j$ with
$w(v_t, v_{t+1}) > 0$ at each step.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Blend coloring]
A \emph{coloring} is a function $c : V \to \RR$. It satisfies the \emph{blend
condition} (equivalently, is a \emph{blend coloring}, or is \emph{harmonic} for
$w$) if
\[
  c(i) = \sum_{j \in V} w(i,j)\, c(j) \qquad \text{for every } i \in V.
\]
\end{definition}

Since $\sum_j w(i,j) = 1$, the blend condition is equivalent to
$\sum_j w(i,j)\,(c(j) - c(i)) = 0$. Writing $L = I - W$ for the random-walk
Laplacian of $W = (w(i,j))$, blend colorings are exactly $\ker L$.

\section{The Main Collapse Theorem}\label{sec:main}

\subsection{The local maximum principle}\label{sec:local}

\begin{lemma}[Local maximum principle]\label{lem:local}
Let $w$ be row-stochastic on the finite set $V$, and let $c$ be a blend coloring.
Let $m = \max_k c(k)$, so $c(k) \le m$ for all $k$. If $c(i) = m$, then every
out-neighbor $j$ of $i$ (every $j$ with $w(i,j) > 0$) also satisfies $c(j) = m$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
From the blend condition at $i$ and $c(i) = m$ we have $m = \sum_j w(i,j)\,c(j)$.
Since $\sum_j w(i,j) = 1$, subtracting $m = \sum_j w(i,j)\, m$ gives
\[
  0 = \sum_j w(i,j)\,\bigl(m - c(j)\bigr).
\]
Each summand is a product of nonnegative factors $w(i,j) \ge 0$ and
$m - c(j) \ge 0$. A finite sum of nonnegative reals vanishes only if each term
vanishes, so $w(i,j) > 0$ forces $c(j) = m$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Global collapse}

\begin{theorem}[Blend Collapse]\label{thm:main}
Let $V$ be finite and let $w$ be a row-stochastic strongly connected weighting on
$V$. Then every blend coloring $c : V \to \RR$ is constant.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $V$ is finite and nonempty, $c$ attains its maximum $m = \max_k c(k)$ at
some vertex $v_\star$. We claim every vertex reachable from $v_\star$ has color
$m$, by induction on the reflexive--transitive closure of $\Arc_w$. The base case
$v_\star$ holds by construction. For the step, suppose $u$ is reachable with
$c(u) = m$ and $u \to t$ is a positive-weight arc; Lemma~\ref{lem:local} at $u$
gives $c(t) = m$. By strong connectivity every $j \in V$ is reachable from
$v_\star$, so $c(j) = m$ for all $j$; hence $c$ is constant.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No non-constant blend coloring]\label{cor:nonexist}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, there do not exist vertices
$i,j$ with $c(i) \ne c(j)$: a finite strongly connected row-stochastic digraph
admits no blend coloring taking two distinct values.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:main} states $\ker(I - W) = \RR\cdot\one$ for stochastic
irreducible $W$: the Perron eigenvalue $1$ has one-dimensional right eigenspace.
The proof uses neither symmetry nor reversibility, and only that a maximum is
extreme and a convex combination cannot exceed it.
\end{remark}

\section{Sharpness: strong connectivity is necessary}\label{sec:sharp}

\begin{proposition}[Sharpness]\label{prop:sharp}
Let $V = \{0,1\}$ and $w(i,j) = 1$ if $i = j$ and $0$ otherwise (two disjoint
self-loops). Then $w$ is row-stochastic, $w$ is not strongly connected, and the
coloring $c(0) = 0,\ c(1) = 1$ is a non-constant blend coloring.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Each row has a single entry $1$, so $w$ is row-stochastic. No positive-weight arc
joins $0$ and $1$, so neither is reachable from the other. The blend condition at
$i$ reads $c(i) = 1\cdot c(i) = c(i)$, true for \emph{every} $c$; in particular
for $c(0) = 0 \ne 1 = c(1)$.
\end{proof}

Thus strong connectivity is the exact dividing line and cannot be weakened.

\section{The directed cycle: an infinite family}\label{sec:cycle}

\begin{definition}[Directed $n$-cycle weighting]
For $n \ge 1$, index vertices by $\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}$ and set
$w_n(i,j) = 1$ if $j \equiv i+1 \pmod n$ and $0$ otherwise.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:cycrow}
$w_n$ is row-stochastic: exactly one $j$ (namely $i+1$) has weight $1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Strong connectivity of the cycle]\label{lem:cycsc}
$w_n$ is strongly connected: for any $i$ and $m \ge 0$ there is a positive-weight
walk of length $m$ from $i$ to $i+m \pmod n$, and every $j = i + (j-i)$ is thus
reachable.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induct on $m$: the length-$0$ walk is trivial; a walk from $i$ to $i+m$ extends
to $i+m+1$ via the arc $i+m \to i+m+1$ of weight $1$. Take $m = (j-i) \bmod n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cycle collapse]\label{thm:cyc}
For every $n \ge 1$, every blend coloring of the directed $n$-cycle is constant.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemmas~\ref{lem:cycrow} and \ref{lem:cycsc}, $w_n$ is row-stochastic and
strongly connected, so Theorem~\ref{thm:main} applies. Directly, the blend
condition reads $c(i) = c(i+1)$ for all $i$, and chaining around the ring gives
$c(0) = \dots = c(n-1)$.
\end{proof}

\section{Vector-valued and geometric generalizations}\label{sec:vector}

\begin{theorem}[Vector-valued collapse]\label{thm:vec}
Let $w$ be a finite strongly connected row-stochastic weighting on $V$, let
$\kappa$ be an index set, and let $c : V \to \RR^\kappa$ satisfy the blend
condition coordinatewise. Then $c$ is constant.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For each coordinate $\alpha$, the coloring $c_\alpha(i) := c(i)_\alpha$ satisfies
the scalar blend condition, so Theorem~\ref{thm:main} makes it constant. Hence
$c$ is constant.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Strictly convex targets]
The scalar proof used only that a maximum is extreme and a convex combination
cannot exceed it. This suggests the collapse holds whenever colors live in a
strictly convex geometry (a strictly convex normed space, or a nonpositively
curved metric space with ``weighted average'' read as barycenter), where extreme
points are not nontrivial convex combinations of others; extremality is then
contagious and strong connectivity spreads it. Convexity that is not strict can
permit non-constant blend colorings, mirroring \S\ref{sec:sharp}.
\end{remark}

\section{Applications and algorithms}\label{sec:algo}

\paragraph{Markov chains (discrete Liouville).} Reading $w(i,j)$ as a transition
probability, the blend condition is $c(i) = \mathbb{E}[c(X_1)\mid X_0 = i]$ and
strong connectivity is irreducibility; Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is the classical
statement that a finite irreducible chain has only constant harmonic functions.

\paragraph{Consensus dynamics.} Blend colorings are the fixed points of the
averaging iteration $c^{(t+1)}(i) = \sum_j w(i,j)\, c^{(t)}(j)$; on strongly
connected networks the only fixed points are consensus states, toward which the
dynamics converge.

\paragraph{Algorithms.} (A) Verify hypotheses: nonnegativity, row sums, and
strong connectivity via strongly-connected-components search, in
$O(|V|^2)$/$O(|V|+|E|)$. (B) Compute $\ker(I - W)$ by Gaussian elimination and
confirm dimension $1$, in $O(|V|^3)$. (C) Compute the Dobrushin coefficient
\[
  \delta(W^r) = 1 - \min_{i,i'} \sum_j \min\bigl((W^r)_{ij}, (W^r)_{i'j}\bigr);
\]
if $\delta(W^r) < 1$ the averaging map contracts the spread
$\osc(c) = \max_i c(i) - \min_i c(i)$ by that factor every $r$ steps, giving
geometric convergence to consensus and constancy of fixed points.

\section{Discussion}

Essential hypotheses are row-stochasticity (making the constraint a convex
combination), strong connectivity (globalizing the maximum principle), and
finiteness (ensuring the maximum is attained). Inessential are symmetry,
reversibility, detailed balance, and one-dimensionality of the color space. This
robustness makes the result an organizing principle for a family of uniformity
theorems.

\section{Future directions}\label{sec:future}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{\arabic*.}]
  \item \textbf{The collapse has a speed, and the speed is spectral.} Repeated
        averaging drives any coloring to the global average geometrically, at a
        rate set by the guaranteed outgoing-mass overlap of any two vertices;
        strong connectivity plus uniform positive overlap upgrades the one-step
        maximum principle to a strict Dobrushin contraction.
  \item \textbf{Strict convexity, not the real line, is what forces collapse.}
        The collapse should hold in any strictly convex geometry and can fail in
        merely convex ones; at an extreme point the averaging equation forces all
        positively weighted neighbors to that extreme point, and strong
        connectivity spreads it.
  \item \textbf{When connectivity breaks, the answer is counted by sinks.}
        Without strong connectivity the space of blend colorings is
        finite-dimensional with dimension equal to the number of terminal
        strongly-connected components, with basis given by absorption
        probabilities into those sinks.
\end{enumerate}

